{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA (rebar-debug) — proven root cause + attribution\n\n**Confirmed mechanism (runtime oracle).** Both bare-base raise sites collapse to `llm_unavailable` because `error_code_for` (`src/rebar/_errors.py`, branch 7) blanket-maps every `LLMError` — and `WorkflowError` subclasses `LLMError` — to `llm_unavailable`. dbca (potential-powellite-mouflon) carved out `WorkflowNotFoundError`→`not_found` and parse/validation/version→`invalid_input`, but deliberately left the bare `WorkflowError` base on branch 7 to protect execute-time genuine outages (AC3). The two sites here raise the **bare base**, so they inherit that mapping. Runtime confirmation:\n```\nerror_code_for(WorkflowError(\"unknown scripted step 'foo' ...\")) -> llm_unavailable\n_envelope_error(...)  -> llm_unavailable   (generic MCP guard surface)\n_structured_llm_failure(...)['error'] -> llm_unavailable   (LLM-tier gate surface)\n```\n\n**Site 1 — executor.py:541 `unknown scripted step` — REACHABLE defect, will FIX as `invalid_input`.**\n- The linter (`lint_refs.py`) validates step shape/fields but NOT scripted-step registry membership, so an unknown `uses:` name passes lint and is only caught at execute time by this raise. Confirmed.\n- Reaches the classification seam via the generic MCP guard (`_envelope_error`) and the LLM-tier gate surface (`_structured_llm_failure` — used by review_plan/verify_completion/review_code/scan_spec, which run scripted-step workflows).\n- **Legitimacy = `defect`.** Violates the cited dbca contract: a workflow that WAS found but is invalid (references a nonexistent step) is a caller/plan-authoring fault = `invalid_input`, exactly like `WorkflowValidationError`→`invalid_input` (dbca `_workflow_caller_code`; docs/mcp-reference.md Failure shape). `invalid_input` is already in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` — no new vocabulary, no design decision. This restores correct classification and keeps the execute base → `llm_unavailable` (AC3).\n\n**Site 2 — editor.py:475 `editor front-end bundle is missing` — ESCALATED (design decision + not observably misclassified).**\n- Reachability: `edit_workflow` is **CLI-only** (no MCP tool; grep confirms). The CLI `_workflow_edit` (`_cli/_workflow_commands.py:128`) catches `WorkflowError` and prints the raw `Error: <msg>` — it never routes through `error_code_for`. So on the only surface where it surfaces, it is **not** classified as `llm_unavailable`; the misclassification is latent.\n- Correct code is genuinely ambiguous (a missing local build artifact — neither `invalid_input` nor caller-`not_found` cleanly fit; may warrant a NEW code). The operator directed this be escalated, not decided inline. Per the /rebar-debug not-a-bug + design-decision guard, **do NOT invent a new code inline** → split to a follow-up for the operator's decision.\n\n**Sibling found (Step 7 construct sweep).** Only two bare `raise WorkflowError(` sites exist in `src/` (both named above). Additionally `ExpressionError(WorkflowError)` (executor.py:251; raised from interpreter.py/executor.py for execute-time wiring/output-resolution failures) also classifies as `llm_unavailable`. Whether an execute-time expression-resolution failure should stay `llm_unavailable` (execute base, AC3) or become `invalid_input` is another design decision → filed as a follow-up rather than fixed inline.\n\n**Defect origin / caused_by = `8a31-e1c3-cf31-4239`, class `plan_defect`.** Same mechanism and same attribution as dbca (which closed `--class plan_defect --caused-by 8a31`). Verified by `git log -S`: the blanket `LLMError`→`llm_unavailable` branch was introduced by commit `77f1c4011c83` (\"8a31: MCP failures get CLI's structured error id\", 2026-08-08). The raise sites predate it (executor `7f425fac` 2026-06-18; editor `495dd7c8` 2026-06-22) and were correct-at-the-time — an unmet-obligation: 8a31 created the classification obligation and the pre-existing raise sites never met it. A reviewer of 8a31 could plausibly have caught that not every `WorkflowError` is an outage."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788045290416454714,"uuid":"bef1bec1-e07e-41a6-9761-e21d253ff0c6"}